Here's an implementation of the program you described using the strategy design pattern:
```
import java.util.Arrays;
interface MaximumFinder {
    int findMaximum(int[] arr);
}
class DescendingSortMaximumFinder implements MaximumFinder {
    public int findMaximum(int[] arr) {
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        return arr[arr.length - 1];
    }
}
class FirstOccurrenceMaximumFinder implements MaximumFinder {
    public int findMaximum(int[] arr) {
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int maxIndex = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] != null && arr[i] > max) {
                max = arr[i];
                maxIndex = i;
            }
        }
        return maxIndex;
    }
}
class AbsoluteValueMaximumFinder implements MaximumFinder {
    public int findMaximum(int[] arr) {
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] != null && Math.abs(arr[i]) > max) {
                max = Math.abs(arr[i]);
            }
        }
        return max;
    }
}
class MaximumFinderContext {
    private MaximumFinder maximumFinder;
    public MaximumFinderContext(MaximumFinder maximumFinder) {
        this.maximumFinder = maximumFinder;
    }
    public int findMaximum(int[] arr) {
        return maximumFinder.findMaximum(arr);
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        MaximumFinderContext context = new MaximumFinderContext(new DescendingSortMaximumFinder());
        int max = context.findMaximum(arr);
        System.out.println("Maximum number: " + max);
        arr = new int[] {5, 4, 3, 2, 1};
        context = new MaximumFinderContext(new FirstOccurrenceMaximumFinder());
        int maxIndex = context.findMaximum(arr);
        System.out.println("Index of maximum number: " + maxIndex);
        arr = new int[] {-1, -2, -3, -4, -5};
        context = new MaximumFinderContext(new AbsoluteValueMaximumFinder());
        max = context.findMaximum(arr);
        System.out.println("Absolute value of maximum number: " + max);
        arr = new int[] {1, 2, null, 4, 5};
        context = new MaximumFinderContext(new FirstOccurrenceMaximumFinder());
        maxIndex = context.findMaximum(arr);
        if (maxIndex == -1) {
            System.out.println("Error: Array contains null values");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Index of maximum number: " + maxIndex);
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, we define the `MaximumFinder` interface, which has a single method `findMaximum` that takes an integer array and returns the maximum number according to the specific strategy implemented by the class that implements this interface.
We then define three classes that implement the `MaximumFinder` interface:
- `DescendingSortMaximumFinder`: sorts the array in descending order and returns the last element as the maximum number.
- `FirstOccurrenceMaximumFinder`: iterates through the array and returns the index of the first occurrence of the maximum number.
- `AbsoluteValueMaximumFinder`: returns the absolute value of the maximum number in the array.
Finally, we define a `MaximumFinderContext` class that takes an instance of a `MaximumFinder` object and provides a `findMaximum` method that delegates the actual finding of the maximum number to the `MaximumFinder` object passed to it.
In the `main` method, we create instances of `MaximumFinderContext` with different `MaximumFinder` objects and call the `findMaximum` method on them with different arrays to demonstrate the different strategies. We also handle the case where the array contains null values by returning an error message.

