Determining the point of view of a given text can be a challenging task, especially when there are multiple narrators involved. One way to approach this problem is to analyze the language used in the text and identify the pronouns that are used to refer to the characters and events.
Here is an example code in C++ that can handle ambiguous perspectives:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    string text = "John saw Mary walking down the street. She looked happy.";
    vector<string> pronouns = {"he", "she", "it", "they", "we", "you"};
    // Split the text into sentences
    vector<string> sentences;
    string sentence = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < text.length(); i++) {
        if (text[i] == '.' || text[i] == '?' || text[i] == '!') {
            sentence += text[i];
            sentences.push_back(sentence);
            sentence = "";
        } else {
            sentence += text[i];
        }
    }
    // Analyze each sentence to determine the point of view
    for (int i = 0; i < sentences.size(); i++) {
        string s = sentences[i];
        int count = 0;
        string pov = "";
        for (int j = 0; j < s.length(); j++) {
            if (s[j] == ' ') {
                string word = s.substr(count, j - count);
                if (pronouns.find(word) != pronouns.end()) {
                    if (pov == "") {
                        pov = word;
                    } else if (pov != word) {
                        pov = "ambiguous";
                        break;
                    }
                }
                count = j + 1;
            }
        }
        cout << "Sentence " << i+1 << " is written from the point of view of: " << pov << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define a list of pronouns that we want to look for in the text. We then split the text into sentences using punctuation marks as delimiters. For each sentence, we analyze the words to determine which pronoun is used to refer to the characters and events. If there is only one pronoun used, we can determine the point of view of the sentence. If there are multiple pronouns used, we mark the sentence as ambiguous. 
Note that this code is a simple example and may not work for all cases. It is important to consider the context and other factors when analyzing the point of view of a given text.

To set a countdown timer on an HTML page, you can use the "setInterval" function in JavaScript. Here is an example code snippet that sets a countdown timer for 10 minutes and includes an audio alert when the timer reaches zero:
```
// Set the countdown time in minutes
var countdownTime = 10;
// Convert minutes to seconds
var countdownSeconds = countdownTime * 60;
// Set the interval for updating the timer
var countdownInterval = setInterval(updateCountdown, 1000);
// Function to update the countdown timer
function updateCountdown() {
  // Convert seconds to minutes and seconds
  var minutes = Math.floor(countdownSeconds / 60);
  var seconds = countdownSeconds % 60;
  // Display the countdown timer on the HTML page
  document.getElementById("countdown-timer").innerHTML = minutes + ":" + seconds;
  // Decrement the countdown seconds
  countdownSeconds--;
  // Check if the countdown timer has reached zero
  if (countdownSeconds < 0) {
    // Stop the interval for updating the timer
    clearInterval(countdownInterval);
    // Play an audio alert
    var audio = new Audio('alert.mp3');
    audio.play();
  }
}
```
In this example, the countdown time is set to 10 minutes (600 seconds). The countdown interval is set to update every 1000 milliseconds (1 second). The "updateCountdown" function is called every second to update the countdown timer on the HTML page and decrement the countdown seconds. When the countdown timer reaches zero, the interval is stopped and an audio alert is played using the "Audio" constructor in JavaScript. You can customize the audio alert by replacing "alert.mp3" with the path to your audio file.

